{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'file cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/engine.tar; cat cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/build-record.json; cat cc/engine_handoff/README.md'","aggregated_output":"cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/engine.tar: POSIX tar archive\n{\n  \"image_config_id\": \"sha256:f3428dc085e0800c14b72be27aa43110a21c4abfe380501ee9ffe29a465fa572\",\n  \"archive_sha256\": \"c55b7e4e07f28dc721ca49317d149c6b1c273dd3b658c83f294f82d617e5c400\",\n  \"archive_bytes\": 20480,\n  \"executable_sha256\": {\n    \"/engine/run\": \"6e148a66f68c95f6ddb721ce30c72fb7c91b6ce3d57ec9533c66aa5320721ad6\"\n  },\n  \"platform\": \"linux/amd64\",\n  \"base_image_digests\": [],\n  \"source_commit\": \"fixture-not-a-real-build\",\n  \"patch_sha256\": null,\n  \"status\": \"fixture-only\"\n}\n# Engine handoff receipt tool\n\n`engine_handoff.py` inspects delivery metadata before expensive execution. It\nstreams SHA-256 over local artifacts and creates facts, a draft handoff, and a\nreceipt admission verdict. It uses Python 3, stdlib and PyYAML; pytest is needed\nonly for tests. Optional GGUF inspection uses the repository's existing\n`ref/llama.cpp/gguf-py` (and its existing NumPy dependency). No network, Docker,\nGPU, subprocess, execution service, or model tensor loading is involved.\n\nRun from `/home/muk/projects/siminfeng/campaign`:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py facts \\\n  --build-record cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/build-record.json \\\n  --archive cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/engine.tar \\\n  --model-config /home/muk/models/campaign/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct/config.json \\\n  --gguf /home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-bf16.gguf \\\n  --out-dir cc/engine_handoff/example\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py generate \\\n  --facts cc/engine_handoff/example/facts.json \\\n  --workload cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/workload.json \\\n  --out cc/engine_handoff/example/handoff.yaml \\\n  --invocation-argv /engine/run --tokens 8\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py validate \\\n  cc/engine_handoff/example/handoff.yaml\n```\n\nThe example deliberately combines a **synthetic engine archive** with real model\nmetadata. It is not a real engine delivery. All generated files stay under this\nassignment directory. `facts` defaults its output directory to the directory\ncontaining the tool; `--out-dir` selects another destination. Put generation\noptions before `--invocation-argv`: everything after it is a literal argv element.\nNo shell expansion or execution is performed by the tool.\n\n## Facts and generation\n\n`facts` reads a JSON build record, an archive, a JSON HF config and optionally a\nGGUF header. It writes `facts.json` and `facts.md`. Each leaf fact carries a\n`value`, `tag` (`observed`, `declared`, or `missing`), and source where relevant.\nThe report preserves all build-record keys, including extra claims, and flags\narchive identity discrepancies. Explicit HF config nulls are observed values;\nabsent metadata is missing. Source commit, base digests and executable hashes\nremain declarations. The tool does not infer image platform from the host.\n\nGGUF metadata covers architecture, expert count, experts used per token, block\ncount, file type, and context length. A small adapter to gguf-py's reader stops\nbefore tensor descriptor parsing. Its read-only mmap reserves address space for\nthe file, but the reader accesses only header metadata, not tensor data. The\nGGUF is never fully hashed. The header-only test intentionally omits tensor\ndescriptors to verify this boundary.\n\n`generate` reads the tagged JSON facts, hashes the workload, and writes YAML in\n`engine-lab-handoff-draft/0` form. It copies only observed values. The exact argv\nis observed as the caller's requested invocation. Sandbox and evidence constants\nare explicit requirements, not claims of enforcement. Unknown values stay null\nand appear in `unresolved`; no repository, revision, GPU, runtime compatibility,\ntest policy, resource limits or profiling tool is invented.\n\nWith only the prescribed inputs, **image config ID, executable hashes and\nplatform remain build-record declarations**. Therefore a generated draft is\nexpected to be rejected for missing required identities. An intake owner must\ncomplete these identities using separately established evidence and resolve\nunknown requirements before submitting for admission. Do not simply relabel\nbuild claims as observations or erase unresolved entries. This conservative\nboundary follows the assignment's requirement to fill only observed values.\n\nThe supplied HF config serves as the minimal model metadata manifest: its path\nand recomputed hash populate `manifest` and `manifest_sha256`. This binds the\nconfig bytes, **not the model weights or tokenizer**. For a weight-bound delivery,\nthe intake owner must supply a real model manifest and its hash in the completed\nhandoff; these commands do not fabricate one from a partial GGUF read.\n\n## Admission\n\n`validate handoff.yaml` prints one JSON object with `state` and `reasons`.\nIt returns exit code 0 for `accepted` and 2 otherwise. Invalid YAML and duplicate\nkeys produce a rejected JSON verdict. It never emits a test verdict.\n\n- `received` is the initial draft receipt status; validation recomputes its\n  decision regardless of the status written in the file.\n- `rejected` means an identity or policy constraint failed. Null required\n  identities, malformed hashes, archive size/hash discrepancies, a known platform\n  other than linux/amd64, forbidden execution fields, extra mounts, and network\n  access after staging are hard rejects.\n- `needs-information` means there are unresolved entries, unknown platform, or\n  a referenced local artifact cannot be read, with no hard rejection present.\n- `accepted` means all implemented receipt checks hold and `unresolved` is empty.\n\nValidation rehashes the archive, model manifest and workload directly from local\nfiles. Relative references resolve against the handoff's directory, not the\ncurrent directory. It rereads the build record and compares its archive claims\nagainst recomputed bytes, and compares image/executable/platform claims with the\nhandoff. Executable hashes must form a nonempty map, and argv[0] must have an\nentry. Image IDs use `sha256:<64 lowercase hex>`; other hashes use 64 lowercase\nhex characters. Archive byte counts must be positive integers.\n\nMount entries may contain only `input` plus `access` (model:ro or workload:ro),\nor `role` plus `access` (results:rw); duplicate roles are rejected. The validator\nrecursively rejects keys containing script/shell/cmd/host-path/privileged/docker\nsocket markers, including nested extensions and false-valued privileged keys.\nIt rejects shell/script launcher argv, docker socket strings and privileged\nflags. YAML is loaded safely and duplicate keys are forbidden. Neither artifact\npaths nor invocation strings are executed. The validator checks the declared\nlab-team ownership and durable-evidence/crash/resume contract as well.\n\n## Certification boundary and lab integration\n\nAcceptance is a receipt state, separate from job/attempt execution status and\nquality or performance verdicts. The existing `bin/lab` remains the executor;\nthis tool does not create a job, attempt, scheduler, lock or service. It records\nthe lab's requirement for external durable evidence, interrupted attempts with\npartial coverage, and retries as new attempts after confirmed stop. It does not\nclaim to enforce those requirements.\n\nImage contents are not certified beyond the recomputed archive hash and the\nbuild-record claims. The archive is not extracted or imported; layer contents,\nimage entrypoint, executable hashes inside layers, provenance, build success and\nruntime compatibility are not independently verified. The validator checks\nidentity consistency, not authenticity; a jointly rewritten build record and\nhandoff are not detected as forgery. Facts files likewise are not signed or\ntrusted attestations. Keep input artifacts immutable between inspection and\nexecution; this tool does not hold them open for a later executor.\n\nA metadata check is not numerical correctness. Acceptance cannot certify model\nweights, tokenizer compatibility, safety of an arbitrary engine executable,\nisolation, GPU availability/exclusivity, performance, crash cleanup, durable\nstorage provisioning, test definitions, resource enforcement, or test success.\nThe intake owner must resolve the draft's runtime and test-policy unknowns.\n\n## Tests and fixtures\n\n`fixtures/engine.tar` is a 20 KiB Docker-shaped tar with a config, manifest and\ntiny layer. Its executable payload is inert fixture text. `build-record.json`\ncontains consistent synthetic identities; `config.json` and `workload.json` are\nsmall fixtures. They are not real build records or runnable images.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q \\\n  cc/engine_handoff/test_engine_handoff.py \\\n  --basetemp=cc/engine_handoff/.test-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=cc/engine_handoff/.pytest_cache\n```\n\nThe accepted-path test uses a separately completed handoff with identities matching\nthe fixture record and an empty unresolved list; it does not pretend generation\nobserved those identities. Tests cover required null identities, same-size archive\ntampering, size mismatch, wrong platform, mounts, privileged and execution keys,\nnetwork policy, missing/tampered inputs, build-record mismatch, relative paths,\nunresolved admission, fact provenance, CLI round trips, bad YAML, and header-only\nGGUF reading. Test data and caches stay under this directory.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
